
######## Friday, the 14th of March, 1930 (fine and cold) ########
   ◆ Ada's past: He was always a good son to her, and sent half his earnings regularly to help to keep the home together.
   ◆ Arthur's past: He resolved to economise, in order that he might insure his life.
   ◆ Mr. Thomas's past: He was never known to spend a shilling foolishly.

=== half past seven in the morning ===
   Ada thinks: She is anxious to pay the rent.
   Ada [She empties the waste-pap] → Empty the waste-paper baskets and dust the desks.
   ⇒ Ada emptied the baskets and dusted the desks.
   Arthur thinks: The half-crown is still missing, and Mr. Hale is distressed about it.
   Arthur [that is, he talks with hi] → Talk with Joan Hale about what she did today.
   ⇒ Arthur spoke with Joan Hale about what she did today.

=== half past eight in the morning ===
   (Mr. Thomas sets Arthur to the quarterly ledger.)
   Ada thinks: , will be enough, and leave nothing to be added.
   Ada [Ada puts away her pail an] → Put away her pail and go on to Finsbury Circus, so as to be there by nine.
   ⇒ Ada went on to Finsbury Circus.
      Mr. Arthur Hale: "good morning!"
   Arthur thinks: 
   Arthur [— → unreadable, waits] → Wait (choice unreadable).
   ⇒ Arthur waited.
   Mr. Thomas thinks: Mr. Walter Thomas is punctual.
   Mr. Thomas [He passes the time of day] → Pass the time of day with Mr. Arthur Hale.
      Mr. Walter Thomas: "I see by the paper that the Queen has gone to Scotland."
      Mr. Arthur Hale: "I have read in the paper that Her Majesty has gone to Scotland."
      → Mr. Thomas now thinks well of Mr. Arthur Hale.
      → Arthur now thinks well of Mr. Walter Thomas.
   ⇒ Mr. Thomas spoke with Mr. Arthur Hale about the news in the paper.

=== half past nine in the morning ===
   Arthur thinks: He is conscious of possessing a purse containing 4s.
   Arthur [He looks about the floor ] → Look about the floor for the half-crown he lost yesterday.
   ⇒ Arthur found his half-crown under his desk!
   Mr. Thomas thinks: He set Mr. Hale to balance the quarterly ledger.
   Mr. Thomas [— → unreadable, waits] → Wait (choice unreadable).
   ⇒ Mr. Thomas waited.

=== half past twelve in the afternoon ===
   Arthur thinks: He is balancing the ledger.
   Arthur [He does C. → unreadable, waits] → Wait (choice unreadable).
   ⇒ Arthur waited.
   Mr. Thomas thinks: He is punctual.
   Mr. Thomas [He does ONE. → unreadable, waits] → Wait (choice unreadable).
   ⇒ Mr. Thomas waited.
   Ada thinks: Anything about me?
   Ada [She talks with Bert Turne] → Talk with Bert Turner about how the day went at the docks.
   ⇒ Ada spoke with Bert Turner about how the day went at the docks.

=== five o'clock in the evening ===
   (Wages: Arthur receives £3 10s.)
   Arthur thinks: He is anxious to insure his life.
   Arthur [He passes the time of day] → Pass the time of day with Mr. Walter Thomas.
      Mr. Arthur Hale: "I spoke to Mr. Thomas about the news in the papers."
      Mr. Walter Thomas: "He added that the news was unimportant."
      → Arthur now likes and trusts Mr. Walter Thomas.
      → Mr. Thomas now likes and trusts Mr. Arthur Hale.
   ⇒ Arthur spoke with Mr. Walter Thomas about the news in the paper.
   Mr. Thomas thinks: 
   Mr. Thomas [— → unreadable, waits] → Wait (choice unreadable).
   ⇒ Mr. Thomas waited.
   Ada thinks: She is anxious about the rent, because it is due tonight, and she has only three shillings and ninepence in her purse.
   Ada [She buys a rabbit, onions | — | — votes [0,0,0,1,0]] → Buy a rabbit, onions and a loaf, for a stew (1s. 9d.); she would have 2s. 0d. left.
   ⇒ Ada bought a rabbit, onions and a loaf, for a stew for 1s. 9d.

=== seven o'clock in the evening ===
   Arthur thinks: He is anxious to become a member of a friendly society.
   Arthur [He gives Edith 35 shillin | . Hale thinks that, if he | He gives Edith 30 shillin votes [1,0,2,0,0,0]] → Give Edith 40 shillings for the week's housekeeping; he would keep £1 17s. 0d..
   ⇒ Arthur gave Edith 40s. for the housekeeping.
   Ada thinks: She is anxious about the rent, because it is overdue.
   Ada [Cooks the supper and mend | Talked with Bert about th | I talked with Bert Turner votes [0,0,1,2]] → Talk with Bert Turner about how the day went at the docks.
      Mrs. Ada Turner: "Well, Bert, how did things go at the docks to-day?"
      Bert Turner: "Oh, pretty well, mother.  There was a fair amount o' work."
   ⇒ Ada spoke with Bert Turner about how the day went at the docks.

